course: physics
lesson: 20
title: Light and shadows
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Light comes from things that shine, like the Sun, a lamp or a fire, and it travels in straight lines, faster than anything else.
explain: We see most things because some of that light bounces off them into our eyes; in a room with no light at all you see nothing, however long you wait.
explain: When something blocks the light, it leaves a dark patch behind it in its own outline: a shadow, like your hand rabbit on the bedroom wall.
explain: A low morning Sun stretches your shadow long across the playground, while the high Sun at midday shrinks it to a puddle at your feet.
explain: Light things bounce most light away and dark things soak it up and turn it into warmth, and the colour you see is the light a thing bounces back to you.
fact: Light travels in straight lines and cannot bend round you, so your body leaves a shadow where it blocks the sunlight.
fact: On a cloudy day light comes from all over the sky, so shadows are faint or missing.
fact: Shadows are long when the Sun is low in the morning and evening and short when it is high at midday, and sundials used shadows to tell the time.
fact: A shadow shows the outline of the thing in the way, and moving a hand closer to the lamp makes its shadow bigger.
fact: We see a book because light from a lamp or the Sun bounces off it into our eyes.
fact: Light goes about 300,000 kilometres every second, and sunlight takes about eight minutes to reach the Earth.
fact: Starlight takes years, even thousands of years, to reach us, so we see each star as it was when its light set off.
fact: White things reflect most light and dark things absorb it and turn it into heat, so a white T-shirt keeps you cooler in the Sun.
fact: A cat's eyes shine in headlights because a mirror-like layer at the back of the eye bounces light back out, helping the cat see in dim light.
fact: A red apple looks red because it bounces red light and soaks up the other colours; under a blue lamp it looks almost black.
fact: A reflective strip bounces car headlights straight back to the driver, so a cyclist wearing one is seen from much further away.
exercise: Why does your body make a shadow on a sunny day? | It blocks the light, which travels in straight lines | The Sun paints a dark copy of you | Your body gives out darkness | The ground gets cold where you stand
exercise: Why are shadows faint or missing on a cloudy day? | The light comes from all over the sky | Clouds soak up all the shadows and keep them | The Sun turns off when it is cloudy | Shadows only exist in summer
exercise: When is your shadow shortest? | At midday, when the Sun is high | Early in the morning, just after sunrise | In the evening, as you walk home from school | At sunset, when the sky turns red
exercise: Why is your shadow long in the morning? | The low Sun shines across at a slant | You are taller in the morning | The ground is wetter in the morning | The Sun is closer to the Earth in the morning
exercise: How did people tell the time with a stick in the ground? | By watching where its shadow fell | By counting the birds that sat on it | By measuring how warm it got | By listening to the wind around it
exercise: You move your hand rabbit closer to the lamp. What happens to the shadow? | It grows bigger | It shrinks | It disappears | It turns upside down
exercise: How do you see a book on the table? | Light bounces off it into your eyes | Your eyes send out rays that touch it | The book glows by itself | You see it by the heat it gives off
exercise: In a room with no light at all, what do you see after a long wait? | Nothing at all | Everything, once your eyes adjust | Only bright colours | Only things that are moving
exercise: About how long does sunlight take to reach the Earth? | About eight minutes | Exactly no time at all | About eight days | About eight seconds
exercise: About how far does light travel in one second? | About 300,000 kilometres | About 300 metres, like a race track | About 300 kilometres, like a long drive | About 3,000 kilometres, across a sea
exercise: What are you really seeing when you look at a star? | Light that left it long ago | The star exactly as it is tonight | Light that the Moon sends to it | A reflection of the Sun in the sky
exercise: Why does a white T-shirt keep you cooler in the Sun than a black one? | White bounces most light away, black soaks it up as heat | White cloth is always thinner | Black cloth makes its own heat | White cloth lets the wind in
exercise: Why do a cat's eyes shine in car headlights? | A mirror-like layer at the back of its eyes bounces light out | Cats' eyes make their own light | The cat is scared and its eyes glow | The headlights paint the eyes bright
exercise: Why does a red apple look red? | It bounces red light and soaks up the other colours | It soaks up red light and bounces the others | It is filled with red light inside | It makes red light of its own
exercise: Why do cyclists wear reflective strips at night? | They bounce headlights straight back to the driver | They keep the cyclist warm | They make the bike go faster | They make their own light from a little battery inside
exercise: Why are many houses in hot countries painted white? | White walls bounce the sunlight away and stay cooler | White paint is cheaper than any other colour everywhere in the world | White walls keep out the rain | White walls make the house quieter
test: my sis thinks your eyes shoot out beams that hit stuff so u can see. right? | No, light from things comes into your eyes | Yes, that is exactly how seeing works for everybody | Yes, but only in the dark | No, your ears help you see
test: A cartoon cat walks around a dark cellar and sees everything clearly. What is true? | Cats see in dim light, but in total darkness nobody sees | Cats can see in complete darkness | Cats make light with their eyes | Cats see by the heat of things
test: What would happen if the Sun suddenly went out? | We would not know for about eight minutes | It would be dark at once, with no delay of any kind | It would be dark a year later | It would stay light for ever
test: Under a blue lamp at a party, your red top looks almost black. Why? | There is no red light for it to bounce | Blue light dyes the cloth black | The top has gone cold | Your eyes stop working under blue light
test: A star has blown up but you still see it shining tonight. How can that be? | Its old light is still on its way to us | Stars never really blow up | The Moon keeps the star alive | Your eyes remember it from all the nights before
test: You stand in the playground at three times: 8 am, noon and 6 pm. When is your shadow longest? | At 8 am or 6 pm, when the Sun is low | At noon, when the Sun is highest | It is the same at all three times | Shadows do not change through the day
test: oi, what you wearing black for in this heat, you thick or what? What is the point he's making? | Black soaks up sunlight and makes you hotter | Black cloth lets in more wind | Black cloth is heavier | Black makes the Sun shine harder and longer on you
test: A driver says a kid in dark clothes "came out of nowhere" at night. What would have helped? | A reflective strip or bright vest | A black coat and a pair of dark trousers | Walking along with no light at all | Wearing a big woolly hat pulled down low
test: Which is true about light and shadow? | A shadow has the outline of the thing that blocks the light | Shadows are made of dark gas | Shadows are thicker on sunny days at noon | A shadow can bend round corners
test: Why do we usually see an object best when a lamp is behind us shining onto it? | More light bounces off it into our eyes | The lamp heats it up so that it glows by itself | The light pushes the object a little nearer to us | The lamp makes the pupils of our eyes much bigger
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an opaque body casts a shadow. | Light is propagated in straight lines and is obstructed by the body | The body emits darkness in the opposite direction | The body absorbs all heat from its surroundings | Light curves around the body
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the perception of a non-luminous object? | Light reflected from the object enters the eye | Rays emitted by the eye travel out and strike the object | The object emits light of its own | The object is perceived by its warmth
exam: It is given that the light of the Sun requires approximately eight minutes to reach the Earth. Determine what may be inferred should the Sun cease to shine. | Its extinction would not be perceived for approximately eight minutes | Its extinction would be perceived instantaneously | Its extinction would be perceived after eight days | Its extinction could never be perceived
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a red apple presents a red appearance. | It reflects light of the red colour and absorbs the remainder | It absorbs light of the red colour and reflects the remainder | It emits red light of its own | It transmits all light without alteration
